{"track":"a2level","num":"2.01","slug":"exponentials-logarithms","title":"Exponentials & Logarithms","subtitle":"ClipSAT Printable Practice Suite (clipsat.org) • Topic 2.01 • 20 FRQs + 25 MCQs • Difficulty Progression • Cross-Lesson Synthesis","sections":[{"heading":"Part I — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","questions":[{"q":"[Exponentials & Logarithms · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"Given $\\ln y = 2\\ln x + \\ln 3$, express $y$ in terms of $x$. Hence sketch the graph.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","answer":"$\\ln y=\\ln x^2+\\ln 3=\\ln(3x^2)\\Rightarrow y=3x^2$. Parabola through origin, scaled by 3.","explanation":"$\\ln y=\\ln x^2+\\ln 3=\\ln(3x^2)\\Rightarrow y=3x^2$. Parabola through origin, scaled by 3.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.52*x) + (-1)","log(x - (-4)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-3.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-4],"y":[-1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"$(0, 0)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"[About A2 Level · Practice 2] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 4t + 4$, evaluate $g(6)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 32$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","answer":"Substitute $t = 6$: $g(6) = 4(6) + 4 = \\mathbf{28}$. Setting $4t + 4 = 32$ gives $4t = 28 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{7}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 6$: $g(6) = 4(6) + 4 = \\mathbf{28}$. Setting $4t + 4 = 32$ gives $4t = 28 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{7}$."},{"q":"[Algebra: Partial Fractions & Division · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Complex Numbers · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Continuous Random Variables · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.64*x) + (0)","log(x - (-3)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-2.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-3],"y":[0]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":1,"label":"$(0, 1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"[Differentiation · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."}]},{"heading":"Part II — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","questions":[{"q":"[Hypothesis Testing · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Integration · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Numerical Methods · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.64*x) + (0)","log(x - (-4)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-3.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-4],"y":[0]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":1,"label":"$(0, 1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"[Poisson Distribution · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Sequences & Series · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Advanced Trigonometry · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."},{"q":"[Vectors · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is $g(t) = 5t + 5$, evaluate $g(8)$ and find the inverse input $t$ when $g(t) = 50$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","answer":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$.","explanation":"Substitute $t = 8$: $g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45}$. Setting $5t + 5 = 50$ gives $5t = 45 \\Rightarrow t = \\mathbf{9}$."}]},{"heading":"Part III — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","questions":[{"q":"Solve the separable DE $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x+1}{y}$ given $y=2$ when $x=0$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","cross_lesson":"3.01 Advanced Trigonometry","answer":"Separate: $y\\,dy=(x+1)\\,dx$. Integrate: $\\frac{y^2}{2}=\\frac{x^2}{2}+x+c$. At $(0,2)$: $2=c$. So $y^2=x^2+2x+4$.","explanation":"Separate: $y\\,dy=(x+1)\\,dx$. Integrate: $\\frac{y^2}{2}=\\frac{x^2}{2}+x+c$. At $(0,2)$: $2=c$. So $y^2=x^2+2x+4$."},{"q":"Given that $z_1=3+4i$ and $z_2=1-2i$, find $\\frac{z_1}{z_2}$ in the form $a+bi$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","cross_lesson":"4.01 Differentiation","answer":"Multiply by conjugate: $\\frac{(3+4i)(1+2i)}{(1-2i)(1+2i)}=\\frac{3+6i+4i-8}{1+4}=\\frac{-5+10i}{5}=-1+2i$.","explanation":"Multiply by conjugate: $\\frac{(3+4i)(1+2i)}{(1-2i)(1+2i)}=\\frac{3+6i+4i-8}{1+4}=\\frac{-5+10i}{5}=-1+2i$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.64*x) + (1)","log(x - (-3)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-2.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-3],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":2,"label":"$(0, 2)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"A curve is defined parametrically by $x=t^2+1$, $y=2t^3-3t$. Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ in terms of $t$ and the value at $t=1$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","cross_lesson":"5.01 Integration","answer":"$\\frac{dx}{dt}=2t$, $\\frac{dy}{dt}=6t^2-3$. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t^2-3}{2t}$. At $t=1$: $\\frac{3}{2}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{dx}{dt}=2t$, $\\frac{dy}{dt}=6t^2-3$. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t^2-3}{2t}$. At $t=1$: $\\frac{3}{2}$."},{"q":"Given $f(x)=2x-3$ for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, sketch $y=|f(x)|$ and solve $|2x-3|=5$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","cross_lesson":"6.01 Sequences & Series","answer":"Sketch: V-shape with vertex at $(1.5,0)$. Solve: $2x-3=5\\Rightarrow x=4$ or $2x-3=-5\\Rightarrow x=-1$.","explanation":"Sketch: V-shape with vertex at $(1.5,0)$. Solve: $2x-3=5\\Rightarrow x=4$ or $2x-3=-5\\Rightarrow x=-1$."},{"q":"Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin series for $e^{2x}$. State the range of validity.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","cross_lesson":"7.01 Vectors","answer":"$e^{2x}=1+2x+\\frac{(2x)^2}{2!}+\\cdots=1+2x+2x^2+\\cdots$. Valid for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.","explanation":"$e^{2x}=1+2x+\\frac{(2x)^2}{2!}+\\cdots=1+2x+2x^2+\\cdots$. Valid for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$."},{"q":"Express $3\\sin\\theta+4\\cos\\theta$ in the form $R\\sin(\\theta+\\alpha)$ where $R>0$ and $0<\\alpha<90°$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","cross_lesson":"8.01 Algebra: Partial Fractions & Division","answer":"$R=\\sqrt{9+16}=5$. $\\tan\\alpha=4/3\\Rightarrow\\alpha=53.13°$. So $5\\sin(\\theta+53.13°)$.","explanation":"$R=\\sqrt{9+16}=5$. $\\tan\\alpha=4/3\\Rightarrow\\alpha=53.13°$. So $5\\sin(\\theta+53.13°)$."}]},{"heading":"Part IV — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","questions":[{"q":"Solve the equation $e^{2x - 1} = 5$, giving your answer in exact form.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$x = \\frac{1 + \\ln 5}{2}$","$x = \\frac{\\ln 5 - 1}{2}$","$x = \\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) + 1$","$x = \\frac{\\ln 6}{2}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Take natural logarithms of both sides: $2x - 1 = \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow 2x = 1 + \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{1 + \\ln 5}{2}$.","explanation":"Take natural logarithms of both sides: $2x - 1 = \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow 2x = 1 + \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{1 + \\ln 5}{2}$."},{"q":"Simplify the expression $e^{3\\ln x} + \\ln(e^{2x})$ for $x > 0$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$x^3 + 2x$","$3x + 2x = 5x$","$x^3 + e^{2x}$","$3x^2 + 2$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$e^{3\\ln x} = e^{\\ln(x^3)} = x^3$. $\\ln(e^{2x}) = 2x$. Sum is $x^3 + 2x$.","explanation":"$e^{3\\ln x} = e^{\\ln(x^3)} = x^3$. $\\ln(e^{2x}) = 2x$. Sum is $x^3 + 2x$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.6*x)","log(x + 3) + 0.5"],"xmin":-2.5,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2,"ymax":6.5,"asymptotes":{"x":[-3],"y":[0]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":1,"label":"$(0,1)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Solve the equation $2^{x+1} = 3^x$, giving $x$ in terms of natural logarithms.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$x = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 2}$","$x = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 2}$","$x = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 + \\ln 2}$","$x = \\frac{1}{\\ln(3/2)}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\ln(2^{x+1}) = \\ln(3^x) \\Rightarrow (x + 1)\\ln 2 = x\\ln 3 \\Rightarrow x\\ln 2 + \\ln 2 = x\\ln 3 \\Rightarrow x(\\ln 3 - \\ln 2) = \\ln 2 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 2}$.","explanation":"$\\ln(2^{x+1}) = \\ln(3^x) \\Rightarrow (x + 1)\\ln 2 = x\\ln 3 \\Rightarrow x\\ln 2 + \\ln 2 = x\\ln 3 \\Rightarrow x(\\ln 3 - \\ln 2) = \\ln 2 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 2}$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.4*x) + (-1)","log(x - (-2)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-1.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-2],"y":[-1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"$(0, 0)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Solve the equation $e^x - 6e^{-x} = 1$ for real $x$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$x = \\ln 3$","$x = \\ln 3$ or $x = \\ln(-2)$","$x = \\ln 2$","$x = 3$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Multiply by $e^x$: $(e^x)^2 - e^x - 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow (e^x - 3)(e^x + 2) = 0$. Since $e^x > 0$, $e^x = 3 \\Rightarrow x = \\ln 3$.","explanation":"Multiply by $e^x$: $(e^x)^2 - e^x - 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow (e^x - 3)(e^x + 2) = 0$. Since $e^x > 0$, $e^x = 3 \\Rightarrow x = \\ln 3$."},{"q":"Solve $5^{2x-1} = 7$, giving your answer correct to 3 significant figures.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$1.10$","$0.852$","$1.21$","$0.945$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$(2x - 1)\\ln 5 = \\ln 7 \\Rightarrow 2x - 1 = \\frac{\\ln 7}{\\ln 5} = \\frac{1.9459}{1.6094} \\approx 1.209 \\Rightarrow 2x \\approx 2.209 \\Rightarrow x \\approx 1.10$.","explanation":"$(2x - 1)\\ln 5 = \\ln 7 \\Rightarrow 2x - 1 = \\frac{\\ln 7}{\\ln 5} = \\frac{1.9459}{1.6094} \\approx 1.209 \\Rightarrow 2x \\approx 2.209 \\Rightarrow x \\approx 1.10$."},{"q":"The variables $x$ and $y$ satisfy $y = ab^x$. When $\\ln y$ is plotted against $x$, a straight line is obtained. What are the gradient and vertical intercept?","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["Gradient $= \\ln b$, Intercept $= \\ln a$","Gradient $= b$, Intercept $= a$","Gradient $= \\ln a$, Intercept $= \\ln b$","Gradient $= a$, Intercept $= \\ln b$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Take logarithms: $\\ln y = \\ln(a \\cdot b^x) = \\ln a + x\\ln b = (\\ln b)x + \\ln a$. This matches $Y = mX + c$ with gradient $\\ln b$ and intercept $\\ln a$.","explanation":"Take logarithms: $\\ln y = \\ln(a \\cdot b^x) = \\ln a + x\\ln b = (\\ln b)x + \\ln a$. This matches $Y = mX + c$ with gradient $\\ln b$ and intercept $\\ln a$."},{"q":"The variables $x$ and $y$ satisfy $y = kx^n$. The line of $\\ln y$ against $\\ln x$ has gradient $2.5$ and intercept $\\ln y = 1.386$. Find $k$ and $n$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$k = 4, n = 2.5$","$k = 2.5, n = 4$","$k = 1.386, n = 2.5$","$k = e^{2.5}, n = 1.386$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\ln y = n\\ln x + \\ln k$. Gradient $n = 2.5$. Intercept $\\ln k = 1.386 \\Rightarrow k = e^{1.386} = 4$.","explanation":"$\\ln y = n\\ln x + \\ln k$. Gradient $n = 2.5$. Intercept $\\ln k = 1.386 \\Rightarrow k = e^{1.386} = 4$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.52*x) + (1)","log(x - (-4)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-3.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-4],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":2,"label":"$(0, 2)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Points $(2, 3.2)$ and $(5, 5.6)$ lie on the straight line of $\\ln y$ against $x$. Find the value of $b$ if $y = ab^x$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$b = e^{0.8} \\approx 2.226$","$b = 0.8$","$b = 2.4$","$b = e^{1.6} \\approx 4.95$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Gradient $m = \\ln b = \\frac{5.6 - 3.2}{5 - 2} = \\frac{2.4}{3} = 0.8$. Thus $b = e^{0.8} \\approx 2.226$.","explanation":"Gradient $m = \\ln b = \\frac{5.6 - 3.2}{5 - 2} = \\frac{2.4}{3} = 0.8$. Thus $b = e^{0.8} \\approx 2.226$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.76*x) + (-1)","log(x - (-2)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-1.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-2],"y":[-1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"$(0, 0)$"}],"width":230}}]},{"heading":"Part V — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","questions":[{"q":"The mass of a radioactive substance decays according to $M(t) = 80e^{-0.05t}$, where $t$ is in years. Find the half-life of the substance in years.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$\\approx 13.9\\mathrm{\\ years}$","$20.0\\mathrm{\\ years}$","$10.0\\mathrm{\\ years}$","$15.5\\mathrm{\\ years}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$M(t) = 40 \\Rightarrow 80e^{-0.05t} = 40 \\Rightarrow e^{-0.05t} = 0.5 \\Rightarrow -0.05t = -\\ln 2 \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{\\ln 2}{0.05} = 20\\ln 2 \\approx 13.86\\mathrm{\\ years}$.","explanation":"$M(t) = 40 \\Rightarrow 80e^{-0.05t} = 40 \\Rightarrow e^{-0.05t} = 0.5 \\Rightarrow -0.05t = -\\ln 2 \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{\\ln 2}{0.05} = 20\\ln 2 \\approx 13.86\\mathrm{\\ years}$."},{"q":"A bacterial culture grows according to $N(t) = 500e^{kt}$. If $N(3) = 2000$, find the exact value of $k$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$k = \\frac{\\ln 4}{3}$","$k = \\frac{\\ln 2}{3}$","$k = \\frac{4}{3}$","$k = \\ln\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$500e^{3k} = 2000 \\Rightarrow e^{3k} = 4 \\Rightarrow 3k = \\ln 4 \\Rightarrow k = \\frac{\\ln 4}{3}$.","explanation":"$500e^{3k} = 2000 \\Rightarrow e^{3k} = 4 \\Rightarrow 3k = \\ln 4 \\Rightarrow k = \\frac{\\ln 4}{3}$."},{"q":"A hot beverage cools according to Newton's law of cooling: $T(t) = 20 + 70e^{-0.08t}$, where $T$ is temperature in $^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ and $t$ is time in minutes. How long does it take to cool to $45^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$?","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$\\approx 12.9\\mathrm{\\ min}$","$15.2\\mathrm{\\ min}$","$10.5\\mathrm{\\ min}$","$18.0\\mathrm{\\ min}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$45 = 20 + 70e^{-0.08t} \\Rightarrow 25 = 70e^{-0.08t} \\Rightarrow e^{-0.08t} = 25/70 = 5/14 \\Rightarrow -0.08t = \\ln(5/14) \\approx -1.0296 \\Rightarrow t \\approx 1.0296/0.08 \\approx 12.87\\mathrm{\\ min}$.","explanation":"$45 = 20 + 70e^{-0.08t} \\Rightarrow 25 = 70e^{-0.08t} \\Rightarrow e^{-0.08t} = 25/70 = 5/14 \\Rightarrow -0.08t = \\ln(5/14) \\approx -1.0296 \\Rightarrow t \\approx 1.0296/0.08 \\approx 12.87\\mathrm{\\ min}$."},{"q":"Given $\\log_b 3=A$, $\\log_b 11=B$ and $\\log_b 5=C$, express $\\log_b 25$ in terms of A, B and C.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["A+2C","2A","3C","2C"],"correct":3,"answer":"$25=3^{0}\\cdot 11^{0}\\cdot 5^{2} \\Rightarrow \\log_b 25=2C$.","explanation":"$25=3^{0}\\cdot 11^{0}\\cdot 5^{2} \\Rightarrow \\log_b 25=2C$."},{"q":"Given $\\log_b 3=A$ and $\\log_b 2=B$, express $\\log_b 3$ in terms of A and B.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["A+B","A","2A","B"],"correct":1,"answer":"$3=3^{1}\\cdot 2^{0} \\Rightarrow \\log_b 3=A$.","explanation":"$3=3^{1}\\cdot 2^{0} \\Rightarrow \\log_b 3=A$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2**(0.64*x) + (-1)","log(x - (-3)) + 0.5"],"xmin":-2.9,"xmax":4.5,"ymin":-2.5,"ymax":7,"asymptotes":{"x":[-3],"y":[-1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0,"y":0,"label":"$(0, 0)$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Given $\\tan\\theta=-\\frac{6}{5}$ with $\\theta$ in Quadrant II, state the sign of $\\sec\\theta$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","choices":["positive","negative","undefined","zero"],"correct":1,"answer":"Quadrant II: $\\cos\\theta$ is negative, so $\\sec\\theta$ is negative.","explanation":"Quadrant II: $\\cos\\theta$ is negative, so $\\sec\\theta$ is negative."},{"q":"An arithmetic sequence has first term $a=1$ and common difference $d=-4$. Find the sum of the first 3 terms.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","choices":["-10","-7","-13","-9"],"correct":3,"answer":"$S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d) \\Rightarrow S_{3}=\\frac{3}{2}(2-8)=-9$.","explanation":"$S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d) \\Rightarrow S_{3}=\\frac{3}{2}(2-8)=-9$."},{"q":"Given $\\tan\\theta=3$ with $\\theta$ in Quadrant I, state the sign of $\\sec\\theta$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["undefined","zero","positive","negative"],"correct":2,"answer":"Quadrant I: $\\cos\\theta$ is positive, so $\\sec\\theta$ is positive.","explanation":"Quadrant I: $\\cos\\theta$ is positive, so $\\sec\\theta$ is positive."},{"q":"A geometric series has first term $a=2$ and common ratio $r=-\\frac{1}{4}$. Find the sum to infinity.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$\\frac{8}{5}$","$\\frac{18}{5}$","$\\frac{5}{2}$","$-\\frac{8}{5}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$S_\\infty=\\frac{a}{1-r}=\\frac{2}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}=\\frac{8}{5}$.","explanation":"$S_\\infty=\\frac{a}{1-r}=\\frac{2}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}=\\frac{8}{5}$."}]},{"heading":"Part VI — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","questions":[{"q":"Find the remainder when $x^{2}-5x-4$ is divided by $(x-3)$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["-2","10","-10","-9"],"correct":2,"answer":"Dividing: quotient $=x-2$, remainder $=-10$ (check: $P(3)=-10$).","explanation":"Dividing: quotient $=x-2$, remainder $=-10$ (check: $P(3)=-10$).","cross_lesson":"6.01 Sequences & Series"},{"q":"The locus $|z-(3-2i)|=4$ is a circle. What is its center?","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["(3, -1)","(3, -2)","(4, -2)","(-3, 2)"],"correct":1,"answer":"$|z-(3-2i)|=4$ is a circle centered at $(3,-2)$ with radius $4$.","explanation":"$|z-(3-2i)|=4$ is a circle centered at $(3,-2)$ with radius $4$.","cross_lesson":"7.01 Vectors"},{"q":"Given $y=5e^{2x}$, find the coefficient of $e^{2x}$ in $\\frac{dy}{dx}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","choices":["11","10","2","7"],"correct":1,"answer":"$\\frac{dy}{dx}=5\\times 2\\,e^{2x}=10e^{2x}$. Coefficient: 10.","explanation":"$\\frac{dy}{dx}=5\\times 2\\,e^{2x}=10e^{2x}$. Coefficient: 10.","cross_lesson":"8.01 Algebra: Partial Fractions & Division"},{"q":"Solve $\\cos(\\theta)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $\\theta\\in[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$. Find the smallest solution (in degrees).","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","choices":["510","210","151","150"],"correct":3,"answer":"Solutions: $150^\\circ$, $210^\\circ$. Smallest: $150^\\circ$.","explanation":"Solutions: $150^\\circ$, $210^\\circ$. Smallest: $150^\\circ$.","cross_lesson":"9.01 Complex Numbers"},{"q":"Find the point of intersection (with positive imaginary part) of $|z|=5$ and $\\mathrm{Re}(z)=3$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["(3, -4)","(4, 3)","(3, 4)","(5, 3)"],"correct":2,"answer":"$y=\\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=\\sqrt{16}=4$; intersection point $(3,4)$.","explanation":"$y=\\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=\\sqrt{16}=4$; intersection point $(3,4)$.","cross_lesson":"1.01 Functions: Modulus & Inverse"},{"q":"Solve $\\sin(\\theta)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $\\theta\\in[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$. Find the largest solution (in degrees).","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["119","0","60","120"],"correct":3,"answer":"Solutions: $60^\\circ$, $120^\\circ$. Largest: $120^\\circ$.","explanation":"Solutions: $60^\\circ$, $120^\\circ$. Largest: $120^\\circ$.","cross_lesson":"10.01 Numerical Methods"},{"q":"A geometric series with common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ has sum to infinity $S_\\infty=12$. Find the first term $a$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","choices":["11","7","8","-8"],"correct":2,"answer":"$a=S_\\infty(1-r)=12\\times\\left(1-(\\frac{1}{3})\\right)=8$.","explanation":"$a=S_\\infty(1-r)=12\\times\\left(1-(\\frac{1}{3})\\right)=8$.","cross_lesson":"11.01 Differential Equations"},{"q":"Find the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=(4,-4,7)$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["15","9","8","10"],"correct":1,"answer":"$|\\mathbf{v}|=\\sqrt{(4)^2+(-4)^2+(7)^2}=\\sqrt{81}=9$.","explanation":"$|\\mathbf{v}|=\\sqrt{(4)^2+(-4)^2+(7)^2}=\\sqrt{81}=9$.","cross_lesson":"12.01 Poisson Distribution"}]}]}